# The yamlover language - the self-contained spec. Root body: what yamlover is, the name,
# and the five parts (each a subdirectory chapter, ordered by the pointer array below).
# Sources: YAMLOVER.md, URIs.md, SEPARATOR.md, TYPES.md, META.md, CONCRETES.md, QUERY.md -
# progress in ../../DOCSMIGRATION.md.
Language
description: the full spec – principles, the logical graph, pointers, queries, flattening, concretes
- >
  yamlover is *not* a superset of YAML—it is a distinct but closely related language: similar in surface,
  but with different semantics. Because of these differences, it requires its own parser.
  Reading is **concrete-aware**: a `.yaml`/`.yml` file is parsed with YAML’s semantics, a `.yo` file with
  yamlover’s semantics—each is faithfully read into the same concrete-agnostic representation.

- >
  The graph generated (in computer or human memory) when parsing the language is called
  the **logical graph**. This is described in the [Logical graph subchapter](*:logical-graph).

- >
  A text or other substrate that is read to build the logical graph is called a "concrete" (expression) or, simply, the language.
  Yamlover supports many concretes, including JSON, YAML, and filesystem directories. They are
  all parsed into the same logical graph. The various concretes are described in the [Concretes subchapter](*:concretes).

- >
  The main concrete used to express the logical graph is called yamlover itself, or `yamlover/code`.
  Only `yamlover/code` allows to express all features of the logical graph, other concretes
  have limitations.

- >
  This chapter is the self-contained specification, in six parts: the [logical
  graph](*::language:logical-graph)—omnivorous nodes with ordered relations and their terminology
  (the type system now lives in the [meta chapter](*::meta));
  [pointers and queries](*::language:pointers)—paths, bookmarks, and the query language
  (of which these are the only fragment); [flattening](*::language:flattening)—multicomponent
  keys that spell a whole nested document as flat path rows;
  the [concretes](*::language:concretes)—every way a node is physically stored and
  written; the [general
  principles](*::language:principles) from which the rest follows; and the [relationship to
  YAML](*::language:vs-yaml), covering similarities and differences.
